{"review_id":"00001788454764715786-0001485283-000002-cycle-review-2","reviewed_manifest_hash":"sha256:cca621a8619505e4901d632517b637f1307c5421f53b3fc49e196fde70dc4d95","findings":[{"finding_id":"td-handle-path-traversal","category":"security_issue","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"read-file paging handle bypasses worktree confinement (path traversal), and is active even with token discipline disabled","claim":"`read_file` now checks `tool_output_call_id(path)` before any confinement check: any `path` beginning with `.familiar/tool-output/` and ending `.txt` is routed to `tool_output_retention_path(execution_id, call_id)` and read with `std::fs::read_to_string`, never passing through `resolve_within_worktree`. The extracted `call_id` is the raw, model-supplied middle of the string and is `join`ed into the retention directory with no `..`/absolute-path rejection, so a call with `path = \".familiar/tool-output/../../../../../../etc/passwd.txt\"` yields `call_id = \"../../../../../../etc/passwd\"` and reads `/etc/passwd.txt` — any `.txt`-suffixed file the daemon can read, including other worktrees and other executions' retained tool output. This defeats the agent-loop contract's rule that filesystem capabilities are confined beneath the worktree root and that a path containing `..` or an absolute path is refused. The branch is evaluated before the `if !self.token_discipline.enabled { return Ok(hash_outcome(content)); }` guard, so the traversal (and the diversion of a genuine worktree file literally named `.familiar/tool-output/<x>.txt`) is reachable with token discipline off — which also breaks the acceptance criterion that defaults reproduce pre-PRD behavior byte-for-byte.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","hunk":"@@ -61,6 +65,23 @@ fn sha256_hex(bytes: &[u8]) -> String {\n         .collect()\n }\n \n+/// Prefix/suffix of the opaque paging-handle string a bounded `run-command`\n+/// result names and a later `read-file` call echoes back. It looks\n+/// path-like for the model's benefit but is resolved against\n+/// execution-scoped daemon-owned storage (`tool_output_retention_dir`),\n+/// never against the worktree.\n+const TOOL_OUTPUT_HANDLE_PREFIX: &str = \".familiar/tool-output/\";\n+const TOOL_OUTPUT_HANDLE_SUFFIX: &str = \".txt\";\n+\n+fn tool_output_handle(call_id: &str) -> String {\n+    format!(\"{TOOL_OUTPUT_HANDLE_PREFIX}{call_id}{TOOL_OUTPUT_HANDLE_SUFFIX}\")\n+}\n+\n+fn tool_output_call_id(path: &str) -> Option<&str> {\n+    path.strip_prefix(TOOL_OUTPUT_HANDLE_PREFIX)\n+        .and_then(|rest| rest.strip_suffix(TOOL_OUTPUT_HANDLE_SUFFIX))\n+}\n+\n fn random_hex() -> String {\n     use ring::rand::{SecureRandom, SystemRandom};\n     let mut bytes = [0u8; 16];"},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","range":{"start":225,"end":255}}],"remediation":"Validate the extracted handle identifier before resolving it: reject any `call_id` that is not a single path component matching the journal's own call-id charset (no `/`, no `.`, no `..`), and canonicalize the resolved retention path, refusing anything that does not remain beneath `tool_output_retention_dir(execution_id)`. Additionally gate the handle branch behind `self.token_discipline.enabled` so a disabled configuration resolves every `read-file` path through `resolve_within_worktree` exactly as before, and prefer an out-of-band handle argument (e.g. a distinct `tool_output_handle` field) over an in-band path-shaped string that can shadow a real worktree file.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"td-retention-tempdir-exposure","category":"security_issue","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Full tool output is retained in a shared, world-readable system temp directory at a deterministic path, with no cleanup and a pre-creation denial path","claim":"`tool_output_retention_dir` places the full untruncated `run-command` output under `std::env::temp_dir().join(\"familiar-ai-tool-output\").join(sha256(worktree_root + execution_id))`. On Linux this is the shared, world-readable `/tmp`, and the directory name is fully deterministic from two values (worktree path and execution id) that any local process observing the daemon can derive, so command output — which routinely contains build logs, environment echoes, and whatever secrets a tool prints — becomes readable by every local user with default file permissions. Nothing in the change removes these files, so they accumulate for the daemon's lifetime. The change's own test demonstrates the corresponding denial path: pre-creating a plain file at the computed directory path makes retention fail, which silently downgrades the call to the unbounded byte-capped result; a hostile or merely careless local process can therefore control whether retention happens at all.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","range":{"start":225,"end":255}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/runtime_token_discipline.rs","range":{"start":214,"end":246}}],"remediation":"Retain tool output in daemon-owned state (e.g. under the daemon's existing state/artifact directory alongside review artifacts) rather than the shared system temp directory; create the directory with owner-only permissions (0700) and the files 0600, and remove an execution's retention tree when the execution terminates. If the temp directory must be used, create it exclusively (failing if it already exists as a non-directory or is not owned by the daemon user) rather than treating a pre-existing path as an ordinary write failure.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"td-retention-not-durable-evidence","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Retained tool output is neither durable nor reachable from the journal, so bounding is not lossless to verification/review evidence","claim":"The acceptance criterion requires that the full untruncated tool output be durably retained for verification and review evidence even when the model sees a window. What the change actually retains is a file in the process's temp directory, which is not durable (cleared on reboot and by routine temp reapers) and is linked to nothing: `hash_outcome(bounded.visible)` records only a hash of the truncated view, so the journal, the execution-evidence tables, and the accounting row carry no reference to the retention path or to a content hash of the full output. Once the temp file is reaped — or once the execution ends on a different host or container instance — the elided region is unrecoverable, and a reviewer inspecting the journal has no way to discover that the retained artifact ever existed or to detect that it is missing. The added contract text in docs/contracts/agent-loop.md asserts retention is 'durably persisted in daemon-owned storage', which the temp-directory implementation does not establish.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","range":{"start":503,"end":544}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:fbdfa8b3a2a6e917c1b1a2c0f96b3c9bc1c2392de6c99323a7fb47493bc8dba2","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":75349,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-72","revision":"sha256:d0f55e43028fe1978270f7ca7d5e7eae95822349366a52a0ab63a9fdfdb26c67","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-fbdfa8b3a2a6e917c1b1a2c0f96b3c9bc1c2392de6c99323a7fb47493bc8dba2-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Persist retained output in durable daemon-owned storage and record its identity in the tool journal/evidence row for the call — at minimum a content hash of the full output and the storage reference — so a later verification or review pass can prove which bytes were elided and detect a missing artifact. Add a regression test asserting that a bounded call's journal/evidence record names the retained artifact and its full-output hash, not only the windowed result hash.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"td-explicit-range-unbounded-read","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"An explicit read-file range is never bounded, so a worker can defeat file-read bounding with one oversized range","claim":"With token discipline enabled, `read_file` bounds only the no-range case: `(Some(start), Some(end))` validates `start != 0` and `end >= start` and then returns `slice_lines(&content, start, end)` with no cap on the span and no head/tail windowing. A worker that receives the 'specify start_line and end_line' diagnostic can immediately re-issue the same read as `start_line: 1, end_line: 100000000` and receive the entire file, so 'tool results returned to the model are bounded by default' holds for `run-command` only — a file read is bounded by nothing once any range is supplied. The same escape applies to the retained-output handle, which can be pulled whole in one call. `file_read_requirement`'s `has_explicit_range` parameter is dead in the executor (always passed `false`), which is the shape of the gap: the ranged branch never consults the window at all.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","range":{"start":261,"end":312}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/token_discipline.rs","range":{"start":118,"end":140}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:fbdfa8b3a2a6e917c1b1a2c0f96b3c9bc1c2392de6c99323a7fb47493bc8dba2","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":75349,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-72","revision":"sha256:d0f55e43028fe1978270f7ca7d5e7eae95822349366a52a0ab63a9fdfdb26c67","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-fbdfa8b3a2a6e917c1b1a2c0f96b3c9bc1c2392de6c99323a7fb47493bc8dba2-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Apply the same window to a ranged read as to a command result: either refuse a range wider than `file_read_max_lines` with a diagnostic naming the requested and permitted spans, or pass the sliced content through `bound_tool_result` with a paging handle so an oversized range still returns head/tail plus an elided count. Cover it with a regression test asserting that `start_line: 1, end_line: <huge>` on an oversized file does not return the whole file.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"td-worker-instruction-unwired","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"targeted_edit_worker_instruction is never invoked by the runtime, so worker instructions never state the targeted-edit preference","claim":"The acceptance criterion requires that 'the runtime's worker instructions state the targeted-edit preference for files above a configured size'. `targeted_edit_worker_instruction(threshold_bytes)` is added as a new public function, but the change contains no call site outside its own unit test: nothing in `raw_runtime.rs` prompt composition (the stable prefix / volatile task state), nothing in `familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs`, and nothing in the new integration test file invokes it. Because the function is introduced by this change, the absence of a call site in the change is sufficient to establish that no production path emits the instruction; `targeted_edit_threshold_bytes` is validated as non-zero but is otherwise unused at runtime. Enabling token discipline therefore changes tool behavior without ever telling the model that targeted edits are preferred, which is the mechanism the output reduction depends on.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/token_discipline.rs","range":{"start":290,"end":315}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","range":{"start":204,"end":215}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:fbdfa8b3a2a6e917c1b1a2c0f96b3c9bc1c2392de6c99323a7fb47493bc8dba2","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":75349,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-72","revision":"sha256:d0f55e43028fe1978270f7ca7d5e7eae95822349366a52a0ab63a9fdfdb26c67","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-fbdfa8b3a2a6e917c1b1a2c0f96b3c9bc1c2392de6c99323a7fb47493bc8dba2-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Wire the instruction into the composed worker prompt when `token_discipline.enabled` (in the volatile task state, so the PRD-029 byte-stable prefix is not perturbed), and add a test asserting that an enabled configuration's submitted request contains the threshold-bearing instruction text while a disabled one contains no trace of it.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"td-replay-skip-mixed-batch-unreported","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"A per-block replay skip inside a multi-block batch is reported as a plain successful write","claim":"`apply_targeted_edit` tracks only a single batch-wide `wrote_any_block` flag, so `already_applied` is true only when every block was skipped. In a batch where block A applies and block B's anchor is absent while B's `replace` text happens to occur exactly once elsewhere, B is silently skipped, `wrote_any_block` is true, and the executor reports `wrote N bytes to <path> (change_kind: search-replace)` — indistinguishable from a call in which both blocks landed. The exactly-once tightening narrows how often the replacement heuristic misfires but does not remove it: a single-occurrence replacement (a distinctive added line that the model also intends to add elsewhere, or content already present for unrelated reasons) still converts a genuinely stale hunk into an unreported no-op inside an otherwise-successful batch, which the caller and the model have no way to detect. The atomicity guarantee in the doc comment ('never a partial apply') also does not hold for this case: the batch is committed with one of its blocks not applied.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/token_discipline.rs","range":{"start":186,"end":240}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","range":{"start":358,"end":379}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:fbdfa8b3a2a6e917c1b1a2c0f96b3c9bc1c2392de6c99323a7fb47493bc8dba2","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":75349,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-72","revision":"sha256:d0f55e43028fe1978270f7ca7d5e7eae95822349366a52a0ab63a9fdfdb26c67","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-fbdfa8b3a2a6e917c1b1a2c0f96b3c9bc1c2392de6c99323a7fb47493bc8dba2-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Return per-block outcomes (applied vs. treated-as-already-applied) from `apply_targeted_edit` rather than one batch-wide boolean, and have the executor name every skipped block index in the result text so a mixed batch never reads as an unqualified write. Alternatively make the replay heuristic all-or-nothing — refuse a batch that mixes applied and already-applied blocks with an `AnchorDivergence` naming the divergent block — and add a regression test for the two-block mixed case.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"td-replay-heuristic-silent-skip","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Replay heuristic silently skipped a stale hunk whenever the replacement text appeared anywhere in the file","claim":"The prior defect — `working.contains(block.replace)` treating any stale hunk whose replacement text appears anywhere as an already-applied replay, then reporting it as `wrote N bytes` — has been narrowed but not eliminated. The heuristic now requires the replacement to occur **exactly once** (a `}` present twice is refused as `AnchorDivergence`), and an all-blocks-skipped call is reported distinctly as `already applied, no bytes written` with a different result hash, covered by new unit and integration tests. The residual case — a single-occurrence replacement inside a batch that also contains a genuinely applied block, which is still skipped and still reported as a plain write — is now tracked separately.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/token_discipline.rs","range":{"start":186,"end":240}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:fbdfa8b3a2a6e917c1b1a2c0f96b3c9bc1c2392de6c99323a7fb47493bc8dba2","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":75349,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-72","revision":"sha256:d0f55e43028fe1978270f7ca7d5e7eae95822349366a52a0ab63a9fdfdb26c67","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-fbdfa8b3a2a6e917c1b1a2c0f96b3c9bc1c2392de6c99323a7fb47493bc8dba2-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Superseded — see td-replay-skip-mixed-batch-unreported for the remaining per-block reporting gap.","status":"superseded","supersedes":"td-replay-skip-mixed-batch-unreported"},{"finding_id":"td-retention-unauthorized-worktree-write","category":"security_issue","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Retention no longer writes unauthorized bytes into the governed worktree","claim":"Fixed. Retained tool output is no longer written to `.familiar/tool-output/<call_id>.txt` inside the worktree via `resolve_within_worktree`; it is written to a daemon-owned directory outside the worktree (`tool_output_retention_dir`), and the new integration tests assert `!temp.path().join(\".familiar\").exists()` both when discipline is enabled and when it is disabled, so an enabled configuration no longer introduces untracked files into the candidate change's own diff/expected-files evidence and no longer materializes worktree bytes outside the PRD-013 write-scope contract. The relocation's own consequences are tracked separately as td-retention-tempdir-exposure, td-retention-not-durable-evidence, and td-handle-path-traversal.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","range":{"start":225,"end":255}}],"remediation":"No further action for this finding; see the newly filed retention findings.","status":"resolved","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"td-retention-write-errors-ignored","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Retention failure no longer emits a paging handle for an unretrievable region","claim":"Fixed. The bounded `run-command` path now treats persistence as a precondition: the `create_dir_all`/`write` result is captured in `retained`, and when it is false the call returns the unbounded, byte-capped result with no handle instead of a window naming a file that does not exist. `runtime_token_discipline.rs::retention_write_failure_falls_back_to_unbounded_result_without_a_broken_handle` forces the failure by pre-creating a plain file at the retention directory path and asserts the result contains neither an elided-line marker nor a `.familiar/tool-output` handle.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","range":{"start":503,"end":544}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:fbdfa8b3a2a6e917c1b1a2c0f96b3c9bc1c2392de6c99323a7fb47493bc8dba2","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":75349,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-72","revision":"sha256:d0f55e43028fe1978270f7ca7d5e7eae95822349366a52a0ab63a9fdfdb26c67","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-fbdfa8b3a2a6e917c1b1a2c0f96b3c9bc1c2392de6c99323a7fb47493bc8dba2-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"No further action for this finding.","status":"resolved","supersedes":null}]}
I'll start by examining the current implementation to understand the two blocking findings before making changes.
Now let me implement the fixes. First, the handle validation and path-traversal fix:
Now update `tool_output_retention_path` to validate the call id and return a `Result`:
Now update `read_file` to gate the handle branch behind `token_discipline.enabled` and add canonicalization confinement:
Now let's update the `run_command` retention-write block for the second finding (permission hardening + ownership-checked, non-silent creation):
Let's verify the whole file compiles correctly with a local `cargo check`.
It compiles cleanly (the pre-existing `dead_code` warning is unrelated). Now let's check that tests also compile:
All existing tests pass. Now let's add regression tests for the two remediated findings to `runtime_token_discipline.rs` (an expected/allowed file):
All 9 tests pass, including the 3 new regression tests. Now let's run clippy and fmt to match the configured verification commands, and confirm the other test files that construct `SandboxedToolExecutor` (outside the allowed test scope) still compile since I didn't change the struct's field API.
Let me apply the fmt fixes:
fmt applied cleanly. Let's confirm the check is now clean and re-run tests + clippy.
Formatting is clean. Now clippy:
Now let's run the configured `tests-green-crates` check to confirm nothing else broke:
All tests pass, including `raw_runtime.rs`, `openai_adapter.rs`, and `xai_adapter.rs` (untouched, confirming the struct API stayed stable). Let's do a final `git diff` review to confirm scope stayed within `crates/` and only touched the two files.
This is a pre-existing part of the PRD-72 diff plus my additions layered on top — all within the two allowed files. Both blocking findings are now remediated, verified by build, fmt, clippy, and the full test suite (including the 3 new regression tests). No commits were made, per instructions.

## Summary

**Finding `td-handle-path-traversal` (high)** — fixed in `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs`:
- Added `is_safe_retention_call_id` to reject any call id containing `/`, `\`, NUL, or a `.`/`..` segment, enforced centrally in `tool_output_retention_path` (now `Result`-returning) so both the read and write sides share one check.
- Added canonicalization confinement check in `read_file`: the resolved retention path must canonicalize to a location beneath the retention directory before any bytes are read.
- Gated the entire handle-diversion branch behind `self.token_discipline.enabled`, so a disabled configuration resolves every `read-file` path through `resolve_within_worktree` exactly as before — restoring byte-for-byte pre-PRD behavior and preventing a worktree file literally named `.familiar/tool-output/*.txt` from ever being diverted while disabled.

**Finding `td-retention-tempdir-exposure` (medium)** — fixed in the same file:
- Added `secure_retention_dir` (unix): creates the directory, then verifies it's actually a directory, owned by the daemon's own effective uid, and chmods it to `0700` — refusing (falling back safely, same as any other retention failure) rather than silently writing into a directory a hostile local process pre-created.
- Added `write_retention_file` (unix): writes the retained output with `0600` permissions via `OpenOptions`, instead of `std::fs::write`'s umask-derived default.

Added three regression tests to `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/runtime_token_discipline.rs` (a declared expected file) covering: path-traversal rejection, correct non-diversion when token discipline is disabled, and owner-only (`0700`/`0600`) permissions on the retained directory/file. All 9 tests in that file pass, along with the full configured `tests-green-crates` suite, `cargo fmt --check`, and `cargo clippy -D warnings`.
